## What upstream did with ours

We sent no PRs this cycle. The notes' one section with our name, "The zig
plug (Steve Howell's)", is upstream's own change to the plug we contributed:
the network entry streams its reply in ~16 KB batches (reek 27285), so a
672,807-byte reply that ran the depot plug out of memory at 256 MB now
completes byte-identically. Issue 157 (computed-body tables, left open at
U62) is not mentioned.

## qemu (cobblestone-qemu)

    GREEN 5/5 in 13m03s   cobblestone-qemu bcda127, sandbox 20260925T233735Z-u63
    ring plug + fib 39s · zigemit 52s · codexir 5m12s · x86emit 6m09s
    verify   zig PASS · qemu PASS (IR identical to the native tool) · x86 PASS · boot PASS (6765)


Four U63 source changes broke the build; the first three are fixed in
cobblestone-qemu `aa995ae`, the fourth in `bcda127` (master). None of them showed at U62 because
none of them existed there:

1. **`cites Codex chapter Phase Allocator`** is new in SourceText and
   IRChapter (0 such cites at U62, 1 each at U63). Upstream strips it in its
   plug build library. `subjects/bundle_ringplug.ps1` no longer carries its
   own bundler: it dot-sources `codex/plugs/common/plug-build-lib.ps1`, calls
   upstream's `Build-TranspilerPlug` with the chapter lists parsed out of
   `codex/plugs/zig/build.ps1`, and overrides only `Build-PlugCdx` (we
   compile the bundle ourselves). At U62 it bundles byte-identically to the
   old bundler.
2. **`emit-zig-chapter` is gone**; only `emit-zig-chapter-stream` remains
   (it prints with print-uni and restores the heap per def). ZigPlugRing and
   ZigEmitHosted call it. The ring plug ends with a `RINGPLUG-END` line, and
   `plug_run_ring.py` captures in sentinel mode and decodes UTF-8 where it
   used to decode SIZE-framed CCE.
3. **New chapter `IR/IRFidelity`**, cited by opening.codex, cites IRCheck.
   It is added to `bundle_ir_to_x86.ps1`'s middle end.
4. **`check-chapter` gained a tenth parameter, `fidelity : Boolean`**
   (opening passes `flags.fidelity`, False by default). `emit_harness.py`
   passed nine, so the call curried into a function, and x86emit failed at
   `zig build-exe` twelve minutes in: "no field named 'state' in struct
   CxFn1(bool, ChapterResult)". Fixed in `bcda127`, which also makes
   `check_call` and `lower_call` read their declarations from the checkout
   and refuse a parameter-count mismatch at bundle time (U63 passes; U62 is
   refused, as it should be). codexir was never affected: it calls the
   driver, not the hand-written phase calls.

**FOR DAMIAN: the serial ring stalls intermittently at U63.** With the
fixes in place, all four subjects built on some runs and not others. Two
wedges, both reported by ring_compile's stall detector as "DATA IS WAITING
and the guest is not taking it: it slept through the wake":

- the codexir compile, at rpos 2,097,152, in `__bare_metal_read_serial+0x86`.
  The bundle was unchanged, and the next run got past this point.
- the x86emit transpile (15,786,747 bytes of IR into the ring plug), at rpos
  11,534,336. The same step, rerun twice directly from that sandbox with the
  same IR and the same plug, succeeded both times with byte-identical output:
  3,329,030 bytes, sha 973b629e258b2d08.

Same inputs, different outcomes: the failure is flaky, not a
deterministic breakage. Both stall points are multiples of 1 MB, which is the
ring's refill boundary. The read routines (`emit-bare-metal-read-serial`,
`-raw`, `-cce`) are unchanged from U62 to U63. One U63 change near
interrupts is that the process switch now enables CR4.OSXSAVE with XCR0=7 and
saves XSAVE state when CPUID reports AVX, which it does under our
`-cpu max` on TCG. That is a hypothesis only; nothing here tests it. We
saw no stall at U62 in this container: both of its full runs went
GREEN 5/5 (8m58s, 8m47s), and its four reds were all ours.

The green run above did not stall anywhere, and the one before it (which
failed on item 4) did not stall either. Neither says anything about the
stalls, which are intermittent; item 4's fix changes x86emit's IR slightly,
so stall positions are not comparable across it in any case.

## Our Codex under U63's narrower language

The native codexir above is U63's own driver (`compile-frontend-ir`), so it
enforces the new refusals (CDX1070, 1075-1079, 2070, 2099, 2100). Every
resolved unit we own was fed through it:

    safari-codex/units                     54 ok
    curated-tests/cobblestone/units        28 ok
    curated-tests/roc/units                46 ok
    curated-tests/roc/raw                  17 halt, CDX3007 (cite Foreword/Console)

The 17 `roc/raw` halts are unresolved sources, not meant to compile alone;
their resolved twins in `roc/units` are among the 46. So none of the new
refusals touches Codex of ours. roc-apps' ported corpus was not swept: it is
Roc translated from upstream's own tests, all of which upstream compiles
under the new rules.

codexir exits 0 on a refusal; the verdict is a `CODEGEN-HALTED ... first
CDX...` line, on stdout for type errors and on the IR stream (stderr) for
CDX1078. Sweep on that line, not the exit code.
